---
import { sitePath } from '../data/site';
import { isPublication } from '../data/atlas';
import AtlasLayout from '../layouts/AtlasLayout.astro';
---
<AtlasLayout title="Methodology"><article class="page-width content-page"><p class="eyebrow">How to read the atlas</p><h1>Mechanisms with context.</h1><p class="hero-description">Keep the meaning, evidence and limits of each relationship close to the map.</p>
<h2>Read a connection</h2><p>Arrows show directed predicates; a line without arrowheads represents a symmetric association. Its endpoint order does not assert causation. Select an entity or line to inspect supporting, qualifying and contradicting claims. A maintaining circuit synthesized from several studies requires its own review; the presence of a drawn cycle does not establish a single demonstrated human causal loop.</p>
<h2>Explore without losing evidence</h2><p>Routes and collapsible groups focus the diagram. Search expands a hidden group when needed. The complete static outline includes every record, claim and source, including with JavaScript disabled. Empty evidence categories mean no records have been entered; they do not establish absence in the literature.</p>
{!isPublication && <section id="summary-review"><h2>Check the overall scientific explanation</h2><p>Each selected disease has a one- or two-paragraph summary embedded in its graph snapshot. It connects the central mechanisms, then describes evidence limits, contrary findings and therapeutic uncertainty. In the curator, copy the summary and review prompt into an external research tool to check whether the overall explanation represents current scientific understanding within its stated scope.</p><p>The packet includes the exact snapshot, evidence cutoff and linked sources. Record actual search coverage, cited corrections and the tool's verdict. Changes to the graph or summary require a new packet and review. External agreement is recorded feedback; individual evidence checks and authenticated publication approval remain separate.</p><a href={sitePath('/curation/')}>Open the summary review workspace</a></section>}
<h2 id="scoring">Contextual weight and confidence</h2><p>Unassessed values are null and labelled “Not assessed”, never replaced by zero. {!isPublication && 'Local maps may show explicitly labelled provisional editorial appraisals. These drafts identify their assessor, date, evidence references and limitations; they remain uncalibrated and unreviewed.'} Scores are versioned editorial assessments and require calibration before publication; they are not measured probabilities.</p>
<p>Mechanistic weight combines causal confidence (35%), network influence (25%), clinical or interventional validation (20%), disease coverage (15%) and replication (5%). Each component is rated 0–4 with a rationale and claim references. A missing component leaves the aggregate unassessed. The weighted raw score is displayed rounded to the nearest five, with ties rounded upward. Raw tiers use thresholds 20, 40, 60 and 80; area ratios are 1, 1.5, 2, 3 and 4.</p>
<p>Evidence confidence is a separate ordinal tier 0–4 based on directness, human evidence, independence, replication, consistency, quality, context and causal design. Predominantly non-human evidence is capped at tier 2; inadequate quality or unresolved material contradictions cap it at tier 1. Unknown dimensions leave it unassessed. Outline styles distinguish tiers and the inspector supplies the exact appraisal. Neither score promotes an entity to editorial core status.</p>
<p>Calibration needs two independent reviewers, at least twenty representative subject/context cases spanning strong, weak, contradictory and missing evidence, and at least 80% paired ratings within one level. Null/numeric pairs disagree. Both ratings and adjudications remain frozen with the exact scoring-model revision.</p>
<h2 id="source-checking">Source checking and domain review</h2><p>A source check records inspection of the named report and passage for a claim. The Sources page distinguishes full text, abstract and selected excerpt access. An exact quoted passage can verify traceability while leaving interpretation disputed. Domain review separately assesses entailment, study independence, context, causal interpretation and limitations.</p><p>Human genetics, cultured cells, animal experiments, observational correlations and randomized trials support different inferences. The inspector records the study system. An associative finding cannot be upgraded to a causal predicate by scoring or an extractor.</p>
<h2>Therapeutic interventions</h2><p>Rx counts direct targets. Treatment details distinguish binding from indirect effects, trial evidence from development status, and approval by jurisdiction and indication. An experimental drug is not presented as established treatment. Trial success does not determine mechanistic weight.</p>
<p>Therapeutic leverage is categorical target coverage: approved, experimental, withdrawn or unverified direct target; indirect intervention; or no intervention curated in this bounded map. For a relationship, the category describes its endpoints, not efficacy on the relationship. Read the linked indication context and trial limitations. A numerical therapeutic-leverage score is not supported.</p>
<h2>Automatic provisional appraisals</h2><p>New curation candidates receive conservative appraisals automatically. Versioned rules use context-matched supporting claims, recorded study types, functional interpretation, bounded graph connections and explicit study-independence metadata. Qualifying or contradictory findings are retained and cannot be credited as positive support. Citation counts do not establish causation or replication. Unknown study quality and directness cap automatic confidence at 1/4. Missing usable support leaves mechanistic weight unavailable rather than inventing a score. The inspector shows components, rationale and system provenance. Explicit editorial appraisals can replace the automatic draft in a new candidate; calibration and independent review remain separate.</p><p>Therapeutic categories are calculated from the therapies actually linked in the frozen JSON. An empty therapy inventory produces “No intervention curated”; automatic appraisal does not discover drugs or assert treatment efficacy.</p><h2>Literature curation</h2><p>Disease profiles define scope, aliases and search queries. Scans combine indexing/update dates, overlapping windows, known-record refresh and periodic broad reconciliation. Persistent identifiers deduplicate reports. Source outages and incomplete pagination cannot advance the successful cursor. Retractions and corrections remain visible in the review queue.</p><p>Extraction identifies one contextual finding at a time, with its exact source hash, source passage, contribution category and limitations. Insufficient evidence produces an abstention. Reconciliation creates a candidate bound to a named base digest and reports the complete record diff. Model output cannot approve its own claims or supply scores.</p>
<h2 id="publication-policy">Review, history and publication</h2><p>Authenticated reviewers approve an exact candidate revision, content digest, parent and scoring models. A content change invalidates approval. A new release references the current immutable parent for its disease; stale bases, extra roots, cycles and overwrite attempts are rejected. Historical snapshots retain their evidence, context, source and layout. Candidate comparisons remain distinct from reviewed release history.</p><p>{isPublication ? 'This public build reads reviewed immutable snapshots and their current pointers.' : 'This is an internal development preview. Publication validation rejects unapproved candidates.'} The internal curation workspace is excluded from public builds.</p>
{!isPublication && <section id="illustrative-fixture"><h2>Illustrative benchmark</h2><p>Synthetic graph fixtures test rendering and performance. Their entities, sources and claims do not describe disease biology.</p></section>}
<h2>Scientific use</h2><p>Causome supports mechanism exploration by scientifically literate users. It does not provide patient-specific diagnosis or treatment advice.</p><a class="button-link" href={sitePath('/diseases/')}>Explore the atlas <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article></AtlasLayout>
